WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.480
Leah: Background, ethnicity, and
culture can strongly influence beliefs

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.550
about mental illness and shapes
attitudes towards the mentally ill,

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.179
let’s look at some of these factors.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.590
Social status and the concept of
saving ‘face’ in Asian communities

00:00:16.799 --> 00:00:21.350
is very important and a mental health
issue may cause public embarrassment

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.139
and could damage reputations, this
may subsequently affect marriage

00:00:26.139 --> 00:00:28.299
prospects or business transactions.

00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:31.819
Therefore, those who are suffering,
and their families are less

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.729
likely to seek a diagnosis.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.739
Most cultures discourage males from
showing physical or mental weakness.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:41.949
Whilst western countries are now
leaning more to encouraging males

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.419
to seek help, and admit to needing
support, many still remain resistant

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:50.820
as this may show inadequacy especially
in more male dominated cultures.

00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:56.390
Not every culture trusts western
medical practices, instead they prefer

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.890
to use traditional methods and often
don’t see mental health as medical,

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.530
but spiritual involving evil spirits.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.969
Some South American cultures believe
in the evil eye and don’t talk about

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:13.750
mental health issues for fear of
being labeled as “locos” or crazy

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.620
Buddhism and Taoism advocate
spiritual understanding and believe

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.610
mental health problems are a result
of bad deeds in previous lives.

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:27.140
Every culture has its own way of
making sense of mental health and each

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.260
culture has its opinion on whether
mental illness is real or imagined,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.850
an illness of the mind or the body or
both, who is at risk for it, what might

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.570
cause it, and perhaps most importantly,
the level of stigma surrounding it.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.050
Younger people in all cultures are
more likely to seek help, but older

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:54.050
people especially those from other
countries and cultures, are less likely

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.860
to change their behaviour and attitude.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:00.589
This can cause problems and divisions
in families regarding seeking a

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:05.100
diagnosis either restricting them
or making them break from tradition

00:02:05.309 --> 00:02:07.010
and subsequently the family.

